00:59:05,454 --> 00:59:11,454
Dogma is a 1999 American fantasy comedy film written,
co-edited, and directed by Kevin Smith.

11
01:05:00,000 --> 01:05:06,000
The film stars Ben Affleck, Matt Damon, Linda Fiorentino, Salma Hayek,
Jason Lee, Jason Mewes, Alan Rickman, and Chris Rock.

12
01:10:54,545 --> 01:11:00,545
It is the fourth film in the View Askewniverse.

13
01:16:49,090 --> 01:16:55,090
The story revolves around two fallen angels who plan to employ an alleged loophole in Catholic dogma to return to Heaven after being cast out by God,
but as existence is founded on the principle that God is infallible, their success would prove God wrong, thus undoing all creation.

14
01:22:43,636 --> 01:22:49,636
The last descendant of Jesus Christ and two prophets are
sent by the angel Metatron to stop them.

15
01:28:38,181 --> 01:28:44,181
Dogma screened out of competition at the 1999 Cannes Film Festival on May 21, 1999, and
was released in the United States on November 12 by Lions Gate Films.

16
01:34:32,727 --> 01:34:38,727
The film's irreverent treatment of Catholicism and the Catholic Church
triggered considerable controversy, even before its opening.

17
01:40:27,272 --> 01:40:33,272
The Catholic League denounced it as blasphemy.

18
01:46:21,818 --> 01:46:27,818
Organized protests delayed its release in many countries and
led to at least two death threats against Smith.

19
01:52:16,363 --> 01:52:22,363
Despite this, Dogma received generally positive reviews from critics, and grossed $45 million against its $10
million budget, becoming the highest-grossing film in the View Askewniverse series to date.

20
01:58:10,909 --> 01:58:16,909
The film explores themes of angel, wisconsin, dark
comedy, satire, church service.

21
02:04:05,454 --> 02:04:11,454
The tone is lighthearted and dark.
